  "query_preview": "..\n\nA cold front is draped from the Straits down to the Milwaukee \nvicinity this morning, orphaned off by parent low pressure quickly \nracing across northern Quebec. Forward momentum carries the front \nslowly eastward today, with a few nudges from several weak \nshortwaves in the mid-level flow. One of these is passing across \nLake Michigan this morning, tied to jet entrance region ascent \naloft. Shower production has increased along this segment of the \nfront - this activity mainly targets northern Lower MI this morning \nbut also offers a 20-40% chance for some showers in the Saginaw \nValley before noon.\n\nThickness advection via southwest flow and lots of insolation bring \nan uptick in warmth for most of SE MI compared to the past few days. \nMid to upper 80s will be common for a high temp. Meanwhile, higher \ncoverage of cloud closer the front should limit temps to the lower \n80s in the Saginaw Valley. There will be a lull in upper forcing \nduring the day and low-level moisture looks to hold close to the \nsurface front just upstream, so confidence is relatively low on \ndevelopment of additional showers in the CWA. Afternoon convection \nthat develops in some CAM solutions appear to be tied to a \ndifferential heating boundary in the Saginaw Valley/I-69 corridor - \nthis may provide enough of a trigger absent of larger scale forcing \naloft. HREF and REFS ensemble mean SBCAPE and bulk wind shear on the \norder of 500 J/kg and 25 kt respectively would support disorganized \nclusters of thunderstorms with little potential for severe weather. \nOtherwise, lack of low-level moisture and forcing should help keep \nmuch of the area dry and warm today.\n\nBetter signal for ascent works in late this evening and overnight as \na jet streak rounds the base of the longwave trough upstream, with \nits entrance region passing overhead coincident with PVA and height \nfalls between 00z and 09z. The highest PoPs (50-60%) set up \ngenerally north and west of Metro Detroit overnight. Some \nshowers/storms may produce localized heavy rainfall. The front \nadvances farther into SE MI daytime Thursday, with more favorable \ndiurnal timing and another mid-level disturbance supporting a 50-60% \nPoP mainly south and east of a line from Owosso to Sandusky. Similar \ninstability and wind shear progs will bring potential for isolated \nto scattered thunderstorms but limit chances for severe weather. The \nfront progresses south of the area Thursday night with weak high \npressure offering a window for drier conditions. The front is then \nshown to drift back northward on Friday with another window for \nscattered showers and storms.\n\nUnsettled conditions look likely to continue into the weekend and \npossibly beyond as high pressure settles over the Four Corners and \nlow pressure over Hudson Bay maintains an active northwest flow \npattern into the Great Lakes. Still time to gather details on more \nspecific windows of higher probability for precip, but this upper \npattern generally holds in place into early and possibly mid next \nweek.",
